Subject: [PATCH 0/2] Build ports without building their dependencies
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev

Pat asked for a deterministic toggle in oss/freebsd-ports that lets a
port be built without building its dependencies, failing when they
are missing so the missing ones can be reported for the operator to
install [1].

The ports framework already has the toggle. STRICT_DEPENDS checks
each dependency and fails instead of building it. On its own it stops
at the first phase with a gap, though, so build dependencies show up
in one run and library dependencies in the next. Patch 1 wraps make in
port-make.sh, which checks the port's direct dependencies against
pkg info before make runs and prints every missing origin with one
pkg install line. Exit status says what happened: 0 built, 1 make
failed, 2 dependencies missing. A just recipe, port-make, runs it.

It lists direct dependencies only. make missing recurses into each
missing port and lists its build dependencies as well, which a package
install never needs.

Patch 2 moves the freebsd-port skill from "never build" to building,
staging, and running check-plist through the script. When anything is
missing, the skill answers with the list instead of posting.

Tested on this host, as the unprivileged claude user, against
/var/tmp/freebsd-ports:

- misc/figlet: stage and check-plist pass, exit 0.
- devel/rust-script: lists lang/rust, exit 2, in 0.06s.
- sysutils/tree: lists devel/gmake, exit 2.
- net-mgmt/signoz, as posted: fetch, vendoring, and patch pass, and
  the build fails after 38s, exit 1. That is a real bug in the port,
  which the reply to the review covers.

Patch 1 applies to trunk on its own. Patch 2 edits the freebsd-port
skill, so it needs "[PATCH 0/2] Add the freebsd-port skill" [2],
which is on b/dev as "claude/skills: New skill freebsd-port".

